804</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9566977</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Yauchetal1998"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yauch</surname><given-names>R.L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Berditchevski</surname><given-names>F.</given-names></name><name><surname>Harler</surname><given-names>M.B.</given-names></name><name><surname>Reichner</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hemler</surname><given-names>M.E.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Highly stoichiometric, stable, and specific association of integrin &#x003b1;3&#x003b2;1 with CD151 provides a major link to phosphatidylinositol 4-kinase, and may regulate cell migration</article-title><source>Mol. Biol. Cell.</source><volume>9</volume><year>1998</year><fpage>2751</fpage><lpage>2765</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9763442</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn><p>1.used in this paper: ECM, extracellular matrix&#x0003b; MMP, matrix metalloproteinase&#x0003b; PI3K, phosphoinositide 3-kinase&#x0003b; PtdIns, phosphatidylinositol&#x0003b; pTyr, phosphotyrosine&#x0003b; SH2, Src homology 2&#x0003b; TIMP, tissue inhibitor of metalloproteinases</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-wrap><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Migration of MDA-MB-231 cells within Matrigel. MDA-MB-231 cells were embedded into Matrigel and cultured in a temperature-controlled humidified chamber for 24 h. (A&#x02013;E) A sequence of phase&#x02013;contrast video images taken at 335 min (A), 620 min (B), 665 min (C), 690 min (D), and 705 min (E) after the embedding. The arrowheads point to a highly dynamic invasive protrusion. (F) A center point of the cell shown in A&#x02013;E was calculated and a trajectory of center point movement over 24 h was plotted using the Analyze Particle module of the NIH image processing program. An inset in B shows a digitally magnified image of the invasive protrusion. Bar, 10 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Localization of integrins and tetraspanins on the Matrigel-embedded cells. MDA-MB-231 cells were embedded into Matrigel and cultured for 24 h in L-15 growth medium. The cultures were fixed with 1% paraformaldehyde, and whole-mount immunofluorescent staining was carried out using mAb P1B5 to &#x003b1;3 integrin subunit (A, B, and G), mAb A2-VIIC6 to &#x003b1;2 integrin subunit (E and F), and a mixture of antitetraspanin mAbs (C9-BB, anti-CD9&#x0003b; 6H1, anti-CD63&#x0003b; M38, anti-CD81&#x0003b; 5C11, anti-CD151) (C, D, and H). The staining was visualized using FITC-conjugated goat anti&#x02013;mouse Ig. Images at two focal planes (3.8 &#x003bc;m apart) were captured and processed using the OpenLab software package. Arrowheads show localization of &#x003b1;3&#x003b2;1 integrin and tetraspanins on short microvilli-like protrusions. Bar, 10 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Antitetraspanin mAbs induce cellular protrusions on the Matrigel-embedded MDA-MB-231 cells. MDA-MB-231 cells were treated for 30 min at room temperature with 25 &#x003bc;g/ml of either BSA (A) or purified mAbs: C9BB, anti-CD9 (B)&#x0003b; 6H1, anti-CD63 (C)&#x0003b; M38, anti-CD81 (D)&#x0003b; 5C11, anti-CD151 (E)&#x0003b; A3-IIF5, anti-&#x003b1;3 integrin subunit (F)&#x0003b; A2-IIE10, anti-&#x003b1;2 integrin subunit (G)&#x0003b; 7E7, antiemmprin (H). The cells were embedded into Matrigel and cultured under serum-free conditions for 24 h. Quantitative data on the effect of the mAbs are shown in the table. Percentage of cells that developed protrusions was calculated in five randomly selected microscopic fields. A cell was scored positive (e.g., cell with the protrusions) if it had at least one protrusion with length equal or exceeding the diameter of the cell body. All experiments were performed in triplicate. Bar, 10 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Effect of the anti-CD151 mAb on morphology and migration of MDA-MB-231 cells in Matrigel. MDA-MB-231 cells were incubated with the anti-CD151 mAb (as described in the legend to <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F3">Fig. 3</xref>), embedded into Matrigel, and cultured in a temperature-controlled humidified chamber for 24 h. (A&#x02013;E) A sequence of phase&#x02013;contrast video images taken at 5 min (A), 190 min (B), 305 min (C), 495 min (D), and 895 min (E) after the embedding. (F) A digital overlay of A and E showing contours of the cell in the beginning (white dashed line) and at the end (black solid line) of the recording. Arrow indicates direction of the cell body translocation. Bar, 10 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Antitetraspanin and antiintegrin mAbs do not influence adhesion of the MDA-MB-231 cell to Matrigel. BCECF-AM&#x02013;labeled MDA-MB-231 cells were preincubated with antiintegrin mAbs (25 &#x003bc;g/ml) and tested for adhesion to a 96-well microtiter plate coated with Matrigel as described in Materials and Methods. The mAbs used were: P5D2, anti-&#x003b2;1&#x0003b; GOH3, anti-&#x003b1;6&#x0003b; A2-IIE10, anti-&#x003b1;2&#x0003b; A3-IIF5, anti-&#x003b1;3&#x0003b; C9BB, anti-CD9&#x0003b; 6H1, anti-CD63&#x0003b; 5C11, anti-CD151&#x0003b; and 7E7, antiemmprin. The results are presented as percentage of attached cells.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Effect of pharmacological inhibitors on the mAb-induced protrusive activity of MDA-MB-231 cells. MDA-MB-231 cells were treated with BSA (A&#x02013;C) or 6H1, anti-CD63 mAb (D&#x02013;L) as described in the legend to <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F3">Fig. 3</xref>. The cells were cultured for 24 h within Matrigel in the media containing various pharmacological inhibitors. A and D, no inhibitors&#x0003b; B and E, herbimycin B (100 ng/ml)&#x0003b; C and F, cytochalasin B (25 &#x003bc;g/ml)&#x0003b; G, orthovanadate (100 &#x003bc;M)&#x0003b; H, genistein (10 &#x003bc;g/ml)&#x0003b; I, LY294002 (10 &#x003bc;M)&#x0003b; J, jasplakinolide (500 nM)&#x0003b; K, ML7 (1 &#x003bc;M)&#x0003b; L, bisindolylmaleimide I (20 nM). Shown are representative cells. Quantitative data on the effect of the inhibitors are shown in the table. The percentage of cells that developed protrusions was calculated in five randomly selected microscopic fields. The cell was scored positive (e.g., cell with the protrusions) if it had at least one protrusion with length equal or exceeding the diameter of the cell body. All experiments were performed in triplicate. Bar, 10 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Effect of the inhibitors of matrix proteinases on the mAb-induced protrusive activity of MDA-MB-231 cells. The cells were treated with mAb 6H1 and embedded into Matrigel as described in the legend to <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F3">Fig. 3</xref>. Embedded cells were cultured for 24 h in serum-free media supplemented with BSA plus vehicle (None), 25 &#x003bc;g/ml mAb 6H1 (6H1), 25 &#x003bc;g/ml mAb 6H1 plus 10 &#x003bc;M MMP inhibitor BB 3103 (6H1+BB), 25 &#x003bc;g/ml mAb 6H1 plus 0.75 &#x003bc;g/ml recombinant human TIMP-2 (6H1+TIMP), and 25 &#x003bc;g/ml mAb 6H1 plus 100 &#x003bc;g/ml aprotinin (6H1+Apr). The percentage of cells that developed protrusions was calculated in five randomly selected microscopic fields. The cell was scored positive (e.g., cell with the protrusions) if it had at least one protrusion with length equal or exceeding the diameter of the cell body. All experiments were performed in triplicate.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f7"/></fig><fig-group id="F8"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>Activation of the &#x003b1;3&#x003b2;1&#x02013;tetraspanin protein complexes with mAbs induces production of MMP-2. (A) MMP profile of MDA-MB-231 cells. Samples of culture media conditioned by MDA-MB-231 cells were resolved in 10% PAGE and analyzed by a gelatin zymography (lane a) or by Western blotting with mouse mAb 4H3, anti&#x02013;MMP-9 (lane b), rabbit polyclonal antibody 808, anti&#x02013;MMP-2 (lane c), and rabbit polyclonal antibody SE600, anti&#x02013;MMP-1 (lane d). (B) MDA-MB-231 cells were plated on a 24-well plate in serum-containing growth media for 8 h. After washes with PBS, the cells were incubated for 48 h in serum-free media supplemented with 25 &#x003bc;g/ml BSA (control sample, lane a)&#x0003b; mAb A2-VIIC6, anti-&#x003b1;2 integrin (lane b)&#x0003b; mAb A3-IIF5, anti-&#x003b1;3 integrin (lane c)&#x0003b; mAb 6H1, anti-CD63 (lane d)&#x0003b; mAb M38, anti-CD81 (lane e)&#x0003b; mAb C9-BB, anti-CD9 (lane f)&#x0003b; mAb 5C11, anti-CD151 (lane g)&#x0003b; and mAb 7E7, antiemmprin (lane h). Conditioned media were analyzed by gelatin zymography as described in Materials and Methods. Gelatinolytic activities of the samples were quantified by scanning densitometry using the Molecular Analyst software package, and the data are presented as percentage relative to a control sample. (C) MDA-MB-231 cells were plated on immobilized mAbs in serum-containing growth media for 4 h. After washes with PBS, the cells were incubated for 24 h in serum-free media before the samples of the conditioned media were analyzed by gelatin zymography. Gelatinolytic activities of the samples were quantified as above, and the data are presented as percentage relative to a sample collected from the cells plated on antiemmprin mAb. The immobilized mAbs were: P1B5, anti-&#x003b1;3 (lane a)&#x0003b; C9-BB, anti-CD9 (lane b)&#x0003b; 6H1, anti-CD63 (lane c)&#x0003b; M38, anti-CD81 (lane d)&#x0003b; M104, anti-CD82 (lane e)&#x0003b; 5C11, anti-CD151 (lane f)&#x0003b; and 7E7, antiemmprin (lane g). (B and C) White columns represent MMP-9, and black columns represent MMP-2.</p></caption><fig position="anchor" id="F8a"><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f8a"/></fig><fig position="anchor" id="F8d"><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f8d"/></fig><fig position="anchor" id="F8b"><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f8b"/></fig><fig position="anchor" id="F8c"><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f8c"/></fig><fig position="anchor" id="F8e"><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f8e"/></fig></fig-group><fig-group id="F9"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p>The &#x003b1;3&#x003b2;1&#x02013;tetraspanin protein complexes are involved in ECM-regulated production of MMP-2. HT1080/zeo and HT1080/CD9 cells were plated on immobilized collagen type I (10 &#x003bc;g/ml, col I&#x0003b; lanes a and c), or on laminin 5&#x02013;containing matrix, (LN-5&#x0003b; lanes b and d) for 4 h in serum-containing growth media. After washes with PBS, the cells were incubated for 24 h in serum-free media before the samples of the conditioned media were analyzed by gelatin zymography. Gelatinolytic activities of the samples were quantified as described in the legend to <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F8">Fig. 8</xref>, and the data are presented in arbitrary densitometric units.</p></caption><fig position="anchor" id="F9a"><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f9a"/></fig><fig position="anchor" id="F9b"><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f9b"/></fig></fig-group><fig-group id="F10"><label>Figure 10</label><caption><p>Effect of the pharmacological inhibitors on the mAb-induced production of MMP-2. MDA-MB-231 cells were plated on immobilized mAb 8G6, antiemmprin (lane a), and mAb 5C11, anti-CD151 (lanes b&#x02013;g), as described in the legend to <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F8">Fig. 8</xref> C. The cells were incubated for 24 h in serum-free media supplemented with various inhibitors before the samples of the conditioned media were analyzed by gelatin zymography. The inhibitors examined were: orthovanadate (100 &#x003bc;M, OR&#x0003b; lane c)&#x0003b; herbimycin B (100 ng/ml, HB&#x0003b; lane d)&#x0003b; KT5720 (100 nM, KT&#x0003b; lane e)&#x0003b; bisindolylmaleimide I (20 nM, BIS&#x0003b; lane f)&#x0003b; LY294002 (10 &#x003bc;M, LY&#x0003b; lane g). Gelatinolytic activities of the samples were quantified as described in the legend to <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F8">Fig. 8</xref>, and the data are presented as percentages relative to a sample collected from the cells plated on anti-CD151 mAb in the inhibitor-free media (lane b).</p></caption><fig position="anchor" id="F10a"><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f10a"/></fig><fig position="anchor" id="F10b"><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f10b"/></fig></fig-group><fig position="float" id="F11"><label>Figure 11</label><caption><p>A role of the &#x003b1;3&#x003b2;1&#x02013;tetraspanin protein complexes in invasion of MDA-MB-231 cells. (A) Effect of the antitetraspanin and antiintegrin mAbs on invasion of MDA-MB-231 cells. The MDA-MB-231 cells were preincubated with 30 &#x003bc;g/ml of BSA or purified mAbs, and subsequently applied to the upper compartment of the 96-well chemotactic migration chamber. Cells were allowed to migrate towards the serum-containing media through the Matrigel-coated 8-&#x003bc;M polycarbonate filter for 20 h. Cells on the bottom side of the filter were counted on six to eight randomly selected microscopic fields. The data are presented as percentage relative to a control (BSA-containing) sample. The mAbs used were: A2-IIE10, anti-&#x003b1;2 integrin&#x0003b; A3-IIF5, anti-&#x003b1;3 integrin&#x0003b; A6-BB, anti-&#x003b1;6 integrin&#x0003b; 6H1, anti-CD63&#x0003b; M38, anti-CD81&#x0003b; C9-BB, anti-CD9&#x0003b; 5C11, anti-CD151&#x0003b; and 7E7, antiemmprin. (B) Effect of pharmacological inhibitors on invasion of MDA-MB-231 cells induced by the anti-CD151 mAb. The MDA-MB-231 cells were pretreated with the anti-CD151 mAb as in A in the presence of various pharmacological inhibitors. Cellular invasion through Matrigel was analyzed as described in A. OR, orthovanadate (100 &#x003bc;M)&#x0003b; HB, herbimycin B (100 ng/ml)&#x0003b; GEN, genistein (10 &#x003bc;g/ml)&#x0003b; KT, KT5720 (100 nM)&#x0003b; BIS, bisindolylmaleimide I (20 nM)&#x0003b; ML, ML7 (1 &#x003bc;M)&#x0003b; LY, LY294002 (10 &#x003bc;M)&#x0003b; CYTO, cytochalasin B (25 &#x003bc;g/ml)&#x0003b; JAS, jasplakinolide (500 nM). (C) Effect of MMP inhibitors on invasion of MDA-MB-231 cells induced by the antitetraspanin and anti-&#x003b1;3 mAbs. The MDA-MB-231 cells were pretreated with the mAb as in A either in the absence (white bars) or in the presence of MMP inhibitors: BB 3103, 10 &#x003bc;M MMP, black bars&#x0003b; recombinant human TIMP-2, 0.75 &#x003bc;g/ml, hatched bars. Cellular invasion through Matrigel was analyzed as described in A. All of the above experiments were carried out in triplicates (e.g., three parallels for each treatment). All of the experiments were repeated at least three times. Error bars represent standard deviations of the ratios calculated in separate experiments.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f11"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F12"><label>Figure 12</label><caption><p>A role of &#x003b1;3&#x003b2;1&#x02013;tetraspanin complexes in activation of PI3K. (A and B) Serum-starved MDA-MB-231 cells were plated on the immobilized mAbs or ECM ligands (A, lane f, collagen I&#x0003b; and lane g, laminin 5&#x02013;containing ECM), or kept in suspension (SUS) for 1 h. The cells were lysed and the lysates were subjected to immunoprecipitation (IP) with anti-pTyr mAb (4G10), anti-p85 mAb (U13), or control mAb (187.1). The coprecipitated material was analyzed for PI3K activity using an in vitro kinase assay as described in Materials and Methods. The positions of <sup>32</sup>P-phosphatidylinositol are indicated. The mAbs used were: A2-VIIC6, anti-&#x003b1;2 integrin (A, lane d)&#x0003b; P1B5, anti-&#x003b1;3 integrin (A, lane e)&#x0003b; C9-BB, anti-CD9 (B, lane d)&#x0003b; 6H1, anti-CD63 (B, lane e)&#x0003b; M38, anti-CD81 (B, lane f)&#x0003b; and 8G6, antiemmprin (B, lane g). (C) The amounts of <sup>32</sup>P-PI in the samples were quantified using a PhosphorImager (Molecular Dynamics). (D) Serum-starved MDA-MB-231 cells were plated on immobilized mAbs as above. The cellular lysates were prepared and the proteins were resolved in 10% SDS-PAGE. Phosphorylation of c-Akt in the lysates was analyzed by Western blotting with a mixture of anti&#x02013;phospho-specific (<sup>308</sup>Thr and <sup>473</sup>Ser) c-Akt antibodies (upper panel). The total amounts of c-Akt in the lysates were visualized using control anti&#x02013;c-Akt antibody (lower panel). (E) Densitometric measurements of the experiment shown in D.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9902091.f12"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>